          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em lax 2-adjunction
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            ‘up to adjointness’. A 1-categorical adjunction is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphism"} natural isomorphism
            of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_{A,B} : D(F A,B) \cong C(A,G B).
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-adjunction"} strict 2-adjunction
            is analogous, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C,D
            are instead
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-category"} strict 2-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F,G
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+2-functor"} strict 2-functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            is a strictly 2-natural isomorphism of hom-categories, while a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biadjunction"} biadjunction
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudoadjunction"} pseudoadjunction
            allows all of these to be “weak up to isomorphism”.
          %p
            A
            %strong lax 2-adjunction
            is instead given by a (suitably natural) pair of families of functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ,
                      %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat\phi_{A,B} : D(F A,B) \overset{\to}{\leftarrow} C(A,G B) :
                  \\check\phi_{A,B}
          %p
            such that for each
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            we have an
            %em adjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat\phi_{A,B} \dashv \check\phi_{A,B}
          %p
            It is not entirely clear that the term “lax 2-adjunction” is appropriate; in particular it is not an instance of the general notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+algebras"} lax algebras
            %a{:href => "#Gray"} (Gray)
            and “local adjunction”
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BP"} (Betti-Power)
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Just as the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            makes sense not just in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            but in any 2-category, lax 2-adjunctions can be defined in any (possibly weak)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-category"} 3-category
            when the above is suitably reformulated by using the 2-categorical
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo ,
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C,D
            and 1-cells
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F:C\to D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G:D\to C
            in any 3-category, we say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            %strong lax left adjoint
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            if we have
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                2-cells
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi η
                      %mo :
                      %mi C
                      %mo ⇒
                      %mi G
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta:C\Rightarrow G F
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ϵ
                        %mo :
                        %mi F
                        %mi G
                        %mo ⇒
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon:F G \Rightarrow D
            %li
              %p
                instead of strict triangle equalities, 3-cells (called
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em triangulators
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi s
                      %mo ,
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s,t
              %center
                %img{:alt => "Triangulators for a lax 2-adjunction", :src => "/nlab/files/lax-adj-mdfs.png"}/
              %p
                satisfying the
                = succeed ":" do
                  %em swallowtail identities
              %center
                %img{:alt => "Swallowtail identity for eta", :src => "/nlab/files/lax-adj-swlwtl-eta.png"}/
              %p and
              %center
                %img{:alt => "Swallowtail identity for epsilon", :src => "/nlab/files/lax-adj-swlwtl-eps.png"}/
              %p (where if the 3-category is weak then coherences might need to be inserted to make these make sense.
          %p
            When interpreted in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+3-category"} strict 3-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-category"} Gray-category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Gray
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gray
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tricategory"} tricategory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Bicat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bicat
          %h2#further_generalizations Further generalizations
          %p The following remarks are incomplete.
          %p
            One can additionally generalize to allow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            to be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+natural+transformations"} lax natural transformations
            %a{:href => "#Gray"} Gray’s
            original notion of “transcendental quasi-adjunction” did this, with the 2-categories and 2-functors being strict. Lax transformations do not live in any 3-category as usually understood, but Gray’s particular case can be done in the “lax Gray-category”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Gray
                      %mi ℓ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gray_\ell
          %p
            Note also that there is no Yoneda lemma for lax transformations (though there is for pseudo ones). So in order for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat\phi
            to be determined by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi η
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat\phi
            must be pseudo natural in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check\phi
            to be determined by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϵ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %mo ,
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s,t
            will come from the adjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check\phi\dashv\hat\phi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \eta
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon
            to be pseudo natural on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wide+subcategories"} wide subcategories
            that include their components; see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+F-adjunction"} lax F-adjunction
            for an abstract context for such a definition.
          %p
            One might additionally consider allowing the functors to be lax, as in
            %a{:href => "#BP"} (Betti-Power)
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Seely"} (Seely)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat\phi
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˇ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \check\phi
            are both strict in their
            %em first
            coordinate, rather than one in the first and one in the second as seems (to me) to be necessary for a Yoneda restatement.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax-idempotent+2-adjunction"} lax-idempotent 2-adjunction
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+F-adjunction"} lax F-adjunction
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Gray
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gray-adjointness-for-2-categories"} Gray-adjointness-for-2-categories
              referred to a lax 2-adjunction as a
              = succeed "." do
                %em transcendental quasi-adjunction
          %ul#BP
            %li
              Renato Betti and John Power,
              %em On Local Adjointness of Distributive Bicategories
          %ul#Seely
            %li
              Seely,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Modelling computations: a 2-categorical framework
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.mcgill.ca/~rags/WkAdj/LICS.pdf"} pdf
